  "text": "None of the three solutions gives the correct value, as far as I can tell by checking the small case m = 5. I could not run code, so this rests on recalled values rather than a full enumeration.\n\n**The m = 5 check.** For m = 5 the problem asks for |s(S_6)| − B_5, and B_5 = 52. I recall that the number of sorted permutations (the image of s) has values 1, 1, 2, 5, 17, 70, 349, … for lengths 1 through 7. That gives |s(S_6)| = 70, so the difference at m = 5 should be 18. I also checked by hand that s(S_3) = {123, 213}, which matches the value 2 in that sequence.\n\n**Solution 1** uses |s(S_6)| = 68 and gets (m−1)² = 16 at m = 5. Its c_6 = 68 comes from a recursion it does not verify, and 68 disagrees with the recalled 70. So its answer is off.\n\n**Solution 2** claims |s^t(S_n)| = C_{n−t}. At n = 6, t = 1 this gives C_5 = 42, but |s(S_6)| = 70. The Catalan formula is wrong, and the answer −10 at m = 5 is far from 18.\n\n**Solution 3** starts from F(1) = |s(S_6)| = 68, which again conflicts with 70. Its recursion and closed form (m² + 7m − 28)/2 give 16 at m = 5, so it inherits the same error.\n\nSince every candidate formula gives a value other than 18 at m = 5, none of them is correct.\n\nCHOICE: NONE",
